jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.7~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use. No storage type has been run
    against physical hardware.
  * New test file t/14-parsing.t throws missing, renamed and wrongly typed
    fields at every parser in the plugin. Each field name in these clients is
    written from documentation rather than read off an array, so some of them
    will be wrong; what matters is that a wrong one fails safe instead of
    being acted on. It found three defects.
  * Names are anchored exactly. Perl's $ also matches immediately before a
    trailing newline, so 'vm-100-disk-0' followed by one was accepted as a
    volume name and resolved to the same array object.
  * A run of digits too long to be a vmid is no longer decoded as one. Perl
    turns it into a float, and '1e+30' would have travelled inside a volid.
  * A volume listing row that is not a hash no longer takes the process down
    with a Perl type error; the listing comes back empty instead.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 07:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.6~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use. No storage type has been run
    against physical hardware.
  * Every -b test on a device path now goes through a bounded helper. A Perl
    file test is a stat, and stat on a multipath device whose paths have all
    failed while queueing is still on enters the same uninterruptible sleep
    that hangs vgs. The helper also puts back any alarm the caller had
    running, because nesting alarm() otherwise cancels the caller's own
    timeout.
  * volume_resize waits for the array to report the new size before
    refreshing the host side. A rescan issued while the resize is still
    running leaves the kernel with the old capacity, and QEMU then refuses to
    grow a volume that did in fact grow.
  * The removal script now names the storages that will stop working, read
    straight from storage.cfg rather than by asking pvesm, which has to reach
    every array to answer.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 06:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.5~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use. No storage type has been run
    against physical hardware.
  * Two new test files that put the plugin under conditions an array is
    actually found in, rather than the ones it is documented to be in.
  * t/12-adverse.t runs against a real HTTP server that misbehaves on
    purpose: accepts the connection and never answers, stops mid-body,
    replies 200 with HTML, closes without a response, refuses credentials,
    or completes a login without returning a token. Each case must fail
    quickly with a message naming the storage, and must never hang. It also
    asserts that a create which fails with 5xx is sent exactly once: the
    request may have taken effect, and a retry would make one PVE disk into
    two volumes on the array.
  * t/13-hostile.t attacks the plugin's own state files (empty, truncated,
    binary, JSON of the wrong shape), the ownership gate that guards every
    destructive path, storage ids containing path traversal, shell
    metacharacters and non-ASCII text, size alignment at every family's
    granularity boundary, and PowerVault's additive expand.
  * It also allocates from sixteen concurrent processes against one shared
    array, which found a real defect: the check for whether a disk id was
    already taken sat outside the retry loop, so a worker that lost the race
    died on a name it was still free to change. PVE runs allocations in
    parallel, so this is reachable whenever two disks are added at once.
  * A residual-sd-path section was added to TROUBLESHOOTING: after a LUN is
    removed on the array by hand, its per-path sd devices stay in the kernel
    and only show up as EBUSY the next time multipathd is reloaded.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 05:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.4~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use. No storage type has been run
    against physical hardware.
  * The storage API version is negotiated instead of hardcoded. PVE 9 raised
    APIVER twice inside the 9.1 point releases; claiming more than the running
    PVE makes it reject the plugin and every storage of these types disappears
    from the node, and claiming less makes PVE warn on every load of
    PVE::Storage, which is once per pvesm call.
  * volume_resize now knows about the $snapname parameter added in storage API
    14 and refuses it, rather than silently resizing the volume the snapshot
    was taken from.
  * Deleting a snapshot now first removes the temporary clone that was reading
    it. vzdump in snapshot mode takes a snapshot, reads it through path(), and
    deletes it the moment the backup finishes; an array will not delete a
    snapshot something was cloned from, so every such backup would have failed
    at cleanup and left the clone behind on the array and its device on the
    host.
  * Rolling back to anything other than the most recent snapshot is refused.
    Dell documents what a restore does to the volume and says nothing about
    snapshots taken after the restore point; on an array that discards them
    PVE would keep listing restore points that no longer exist. New option
    dell-rollback-any-snapshot lifts it once the behaviour is verified.
  * The orphan reaper leaves alone any device that still has a working
    multipath path. A disk hot-added to a running VM is briefly absent from
    the array's listing while the guest already has it open, and a guest's
    open file descriptor is not a holder or a mount.
  * An outage is now declared on elapsed time rather than on a count of
    consecutive failed polls, and activate_storage records the failure it
    dies on. PVE stops polling a storage it has marked inactive, so a counter
    that waits for three failures would stay silent through exactly the
    outages that matter, and activate_storage dying means status() is never
    reached at all.
  * volume_snapshot_info and rename_snapshot are implemented rather than
    inherited: the base versions read a qcow2 file through filesystem_path,
    which this plugin cannot provide.
  * PowerVault answers the confirmation prompt on rollback, as the CLI
    Reference requires of a script.
  * PowerFlex reports real snapshot timestamps instead of 1970.
  * pve-dell-config-get bounds its mount and umount. It runs during an outage,
    against storage that may be half dead.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 03:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.3~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use. No storage type has been run
    against physical hardware.
  * Cross-checked against the production incident records of the two related
    projects (jt-pve-storage-netapp, jt-pve-storage-purestorage). Nine issues
    of the same classes were present here and are fixed.
  * A delete the array refused could be reported to PVE as success, because
    $@ was read after intervening evals had reset it. PVE would then drop the
    disk from the VM configuration while the volume was still on the array.
  * free_image no longer proceeds to delete when the array fails to say which
    hosts a volume is mapped to. Deleting a mapped volume leaves every node it
    is mapped to with a device that answers nothing.
  * The orphan reaper no longer falls back to a per-volume array query when a
    listing carries no WWID. That was one call per volume on every poll on
    every node. A listing that returns no WWIDs at all now abandons the pass
    rather than treating every volume as deleted.
  * Temporary clones created to read a snapshot are recorded per node and
    removed when the process that created them is gone. They have no PVE
    volume name, so nothing else would ever find them.
  * PowerStore collection paging follows the Content-Range the array returns
    instead of assuming a short page means the end. A server-side page cap
    would otherwise hide volumes and make the reaper treat live ones as
    deleted.
  * PowerVault and PowerFlex wait for an object to become visible after
    creating it, as PowerStore already did.
  * PowerFlex unmaps before deleting in every rollback path, and rolls back a
    clone this node cannot map.
  * The block-device test that follows a device glob now runs inside the same
    timeout as the glob.
  * New test t/11-imports.t: perl -c compiles a call to an undefined
    subroutine without complaint, so a helper used without its use line only
    fails at runtime. One such slip was found this way.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Mon, 27 Jul 2026 01:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.2~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use. No storage type has been run
    against physical hardware.
  * Review pass over every plugin entry point against the Proxmox VE 9.2.5
    storage API source. Nine defects fixed, all of which would have shown up
    in the first hours of testing against an array.
  * PowerFlex enforced PowerVault's 32-character name limit instead of its
    own 31, because the inherited naming code read a constant rather than the
    subclass. Snapshots and linked clones of a storage with a longer id were
    generated one byte too long and refused.
  * Deleting a volume now removes the snapshots this plugin created for it
    first. PVE deletes a VM's disks without touching storage snapshots, so
    'qm destroy' on a VM with a snapshot, and deleting any template, failed
    on the array. The template marker is handled last, so a template whose
    linked clones still depend on it keeps it.
  * The temporary clone used to read a snapshot was named by hand and ignored
    the family name limits, so reading a snapshot could not work on
    PowerVault or PowerFlex at all.
  * A generated NVMe host NQN is now written to /etc/nvme/hostnqn. It was
    returned without being stored, so every call produced a different NQN and
    the volume was mapped to one no connection used.
  * Linked clones are listed under the same 'base-.../vm-...' volid PVE holds
    in the VM configuration, so 'qm rescan' no longer sees them as volumes no
    configuration references.
  * list_images now matches a vollist filter exactly; a prefix match let a
    request for vm-1-disk-1 also return vm-1-disk-10.
  * Host registration is checked at most once every five minutes per storage
    rather than on every pvestatd poll.
  * The periodic SAN rescan survives a backwards clock step.
  * PowerStore waits for a volume it created or cloned to become visible,
    rather than failing on an operation the array is still running.
  * PowerVault falls back to the formatted size string when the numeric field
    is missing, instead of reporting a volume as zero bytes.
  * pve-dell-config-get refuses a storage that is not dellpowerstore instead
    of speaking PowerStore REST to another family's array.

 -- Jason Cheng <jason@jason.tools>  Sun, 26 Jul 2026 18:00:00 +0800

jt-pve-storage-dellemc (0.7.1~beta1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BETA RELEASE. Not for production use. No storage type has been run
    against physical hardware.
  * The VM config backup volume is no longer offered on the dellpowervault
    family. It spends one extra volume for every snapshot of a VM, and a
    PowerVault ME array's volume and snapshot ceiling is roughly an order of
    magnitude below PowerStore's, so the cost is material there. Snapshots
    and rollback are unchanged; the configuration is still recoverable from a
    PVE backup or from /etc/pve on another node.
  * On dellpowerstore the feature stays on by default and can now be turned
    off with the new dell-config-backup option, for an array close to its
    volume limit.
  * Removal of an existing config volume still runs on a storage that has the
    feature switched off, so volumes written by an earlier version are
    cleaned up rather than stranded.

